-- button.flang — one file, twenty buttons.
--
-- The parameters example from SCRIPTING.md, as a script that actually runs.
-- What makes it reusable is that it names nothing in the level: `target` is
-- whichever sector THIS button was wired to in its Script panel, `drop` is how
-- far it lowers that sector's floor, and `latching` is whether pressing it
-- again puts the floor back. Twenty buttons share the file and no two of them
-- have to share a behaviour.
--
-- The latch is kept in `@self.state` rather than in an attribute because it is
-- bookkeeping rather than level data. The floor's height is the real state
-- here, and the level file already records that; a saved `down` flag could only
-- ever disagree with it.

param target ref
param drop float = 3.0
param latching bool = true

def on_interact(:interact:, src ref) -> null
    if ?target == null
        -- Nobody has wired this one up yet, or the sector it named is gone. An
        -- unbound `ref` param reads null and so does a dangling one, which is
        -- why this is the check to write rather than trusting the panel.
        return
    end

    if @self.state["down"] == true
        if not ?latching
            -- Momentary: it opened the way once, and that is all it does.
            return
        end
        ?target.floor.prop.height = ?target.floor.prop.height + ?drop
        @self.state["down"] = false
        return
    end

    ?target.floor.prop.height = ?target.floor.prop.height - ?drop
    @self.state["down"] = true
end
